        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-32" edge="1" parent="1" source="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-12" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;" target="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-13" value="">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-33" edge="1" parent="1" source="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-13" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;exitX=1;exitY=0.5;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" target="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-14" value="yes">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-34" edge="1" parent="1" source="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-13" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;" target="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-15" value="no">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-35" edge="1" parent="1" source="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-15" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;" target="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-16" value="COMMIT">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-36" parent="1" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;size=14;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=6;fontSize=11;fillColor=#fffbe6;strokeColor=#c8a415;" value="THE HOLE THAT WAS HERE UNTIL 14 AUGUST&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;POST /api/v1/payments with body {&quot;amount&quot;: 999999999} called&lt;br&gt;walletsService.topup(user.id, amount) directly. Any signed-in account could&lt;br&gt;credit itself any sum. No bank transfer, no admin, no PayOS — and the ledger&lt;br&gt;entry it produced was indistinguishable from a real top-up.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The money was immediately spendable, and withdrawable through&lt;br&gt;POST /withdrawals. Only the admin approval step stood between a fabricated&lt;br&gt;balance and a real bank payout — and the admin sees nothing but a normal&lt;br&gt;looking balance.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;POST /wallets/topup was worse in a quieter way: WalletsController carried no&lt;br&gt;guard at all, so a route into a function that writes to the ledger was&lt;br&gt;reachable without logging in. It answered 500 rather than 401 only because&lt;br&gt;@User() returned undefined and the code tripped over user.id.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Fixed: the /payments branch refuses and names the PayOS route; the wallets&lt;br&gt;controller takes JwtAuthGuard at class level and AdminGuard on topup;&lt;br&gt;route-guards.spec.ts now fails the build if any route loses its guard." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="330" width="840" x="50" y="1200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ghMxSvpNALkuH9H7qb1N-37" parent="1" style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;size=14;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=6;fontSize=11;fillColor=#fffbe6;strokeColor=#c8a415;" value="Why the wallet is only credited by the webhook&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The ledger account `gateway_clearing` represents money that PayOS is holding&lt;br&gt;on our behalf. Every credit to a user wallet is paid for out of it, so the sum&lt;br&gt;of what we have handed out must always match what the gateway actually&lt;br&gt;received. Crediting a wallet without a matching bank event breaks that&lt;br&gt;equality, and it breaks it invisibly — the books still balance to zero because&lt;br&gt;the fake entry has two sides. It only surfaces when someone compares&lt;br&gt;gateway_clearing against the PayOS statement.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;That is the whole reason the credit lives in the webhook and nowhere else.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The idempotency key is payos:{orderCode}:{paymentLinkId}, so PayOS may call&lt;br&gt;the webhook as many times as it likes. The second call finds the transaction&lt;br&gt;already posted and stops — a flow final, not an activity final: the request&lt;br&gt;still returns 200, which is what stops PayOS retrying forever.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The same key is used by GET /payos/refresh/:orderId, so whichever arrives&lt;br&gt;first wins and the other becomes a no-op." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="330" width="840" x="920" y="1200" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
